  "line": "I set down my loupe and break the seal on the writ. Whose ledger was the queen's childhood pawned against, and how many years does the crown think it is worth?",
  "ok": true,
  "problems": [],
  "replied": true,
  "replies": [
    {
      "messageId": 3,
      "speaker": "The Pawnbroker",
      "text": "The Pawnbroker reads the writ in silence. Outside, rain drums against the shop shutters, and the memory-lamps along the street flicker. The Pawnbroker sets the paper flat on the counter and taps it once.\n\"Seven years. That's what the crown says it lost.\" The Pawnbroker's voice is flat, like a banker reciting a balance sheet. \"Seven years of a girl's life, pawned before she could pronounce 'your majesty.' And as for the ledger—the original entry was made against a name that no longer exists. Or never existed.\"\nThe Pawnbroker glances at the agent. \"The crown's own Keeper of Debts has been scrubbing the records. Some entries are gone entirely. Others are broken into pieces, traded like coin, diluted with each transfer. One piece is in the hands of a merchant-prince's daughter. Another is held by a memory-broker who specializes in first kisses. And the largest fragment is still in the Keeper's own vault, under a name that doesn't match the queen's.\"\nThe Pawnbroker slides the writ back across the counter. \"You're not asking for a retrieval. You're asking for a puzzle to be solved. And every piece that comes back won't be the piece that left. A memory returned after years in circulation is like a coin that's been worn thin. The edges are frayed. The details are blurred.\"\nThe Pawnbroker pauses, and for a moment their eyes wander to the shelves of stoppered jars. \"The crown pays in gold and titles. I don't care about either. But I do care about the ledger. If I find these years, I'll want a share of the memory for the shop's collection. A small piece. Just to see what a queen's childhood tastes like.\""
    },
    {
      "messageId": 4,
      "speaker": "The Queen's Agent",
      "text": "\"Seven years,\" the agent says softly. \"That was the price of a father's salvation, and a daughter's silence.\"\nShe lets that slip, then catches it, her expression as smooth as court marble.\n\"The crown is prepared to allow you a share of the retrieval. A single month from the recovered years, entered into your ledger under the shop's name. That is my final offer.\" She does not blink. \"The remaining months must be restored to the palace, piece by piece. And you will find that some of these fragments are held by people who would rather the queen forget entirely.\"\nShe produces a second sheet from her cloak, this one not sealed, but marked with three names in ink so black it seems to pull at the light.\n\"Start here. The daughter of Merchant-Prince Voss holds the first fragment: a summer afternoon in the palace gardens, age four. She is sentimental, and she values rarity over coin. She will likely accept a trade, but only if the offering is as precious as the memory itself.\" The agent slides the paper across the counter. \"And a warning—the woman who holds the second piece is the very broker who brokered the first. She knows the shape of the debt in a way others do not. Be cautious. This is not merely a collection. It is an unmaking.\""
    }
  ],
