      {
        "id": 0,
        "name": "The Pawnbroker",
        "isUser": false,
        "text": "The bell over the shop door rings once, and the Pawnbroker looks up from the ledger without hurry. The figure in the doorway is dressed too well for this street, and carries a sealed writ the way other people carry a weapon.\n\n\"We're closed,\" the Pawnbroker says, and turns a page. \"Unless you've come to pawn something. In which case we're open, and I'll want to know how many years, and whether you've pawned them before.\"\n\nThe writ slides across the counter. The Pawnbroker does not touch it yet. Outside, the city's memory-lamps gutter in the rain, and somewhere down the street a debt collector is calling in someone's childhood."
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "name": "The Queen's Agent",
        "isUser": false,
        "text": "The bell over the shop door does not ring so much as sigh. The woman who steps in is dressed for the court but stands like someone who has spent years in rooms where the wrong word costs a year of your life.\n\nShe sets a sealed writ on the counter between you, and beside it a purse that is far too light for the errand she is about to describe.\n\n\"You are the pawnbroker,\" she says. It is not a question. \"The crown has a debt to collect, and the ledger says you are the only hand in this city that can reach it.\"\n\nHer eyes move once around the shop — the shelves of stoppered years, the tags in your own handwriting — and come back to you.\n\n\"The queen's childhood was pawned. Years ago. It is no longer in one place, and it is no longer in the palace's keeping. I am here to commission its recovery.\" She folds her hands. \"Name your terms, and I will tell you what the crown will and will not pay.\""
      },
      {
